{
  "abstract": "A ten-chromatic biplanar graph on twenty vertices would contain a 10-critical graph $G$ with $e(G)\\le108$, $\\delta(G)\\ge9$ and no $K_9$. The order-twenty programme on the r/earth_moon forum fixes an independent triple $S$ and splits on the order $t$ of a 9-critical subgraph $F$ of $G-S$. The branches $t=11$, $16$ and $17$ are treated elsewhere; we settle the four remaining orders $t=12,\\dots,15$. First, Gallai's join theorem and an exact edge budget force $F=K_{9-k}\\vee B$ for a $k$-critical graph $B$. A geng census with a colouring hook gives exactly 19 such graphs $F$, and a second raw enumeration (about $9\\cdot10^9$ graphs for $(k,n)=(7,13)$) confirms it. The budget splits the outside set $R=V(G)-V(F)$ into 104 jobs by its missing edges and high-degree vertices. Any 9-colouring of $G[V(F)]$ leaves each outside vertex a list of known minimum size. So it suffices that the small graph $G[R]$, with 5 to 8 vertices, is $f$-choosable from a 9-palette. An exact SAT test with column-lex symmetry breaking proves this for 87 jobs. A refinement closes 35 of the 50 resulting sub-jobs. A rainbow lemma shows that choosability with one list enlarged forces a clique neighbourhood; it closes 12 more sub-jobs by forcing $K_9$. A short hand argument closes the last three: equal and nested colour lists become equal and nested neighbourhoods through colourings in which one vertex carries the ninth colour. Two exhaustive computations confirm this case. All 149 choosability claims have LRAT proofs checked by lrat-check. Every layer was recomputed independently. Together with the published $t=11$ and $t=16$ results and the $t=17$ branch, which is in preparation, this gives that every biplanar graph on at most twenty vertices is 9-colourable. The steps use only the edge bound, $K_9$-freeness and criticality. Explicit density-feasible 10-critical graphs exist from order 21 on, so topology must enter there.",
